The XGBoost regression model was trained on 140 SPARC galaxies using 5-fold cross-validation. On average, the model achieved a mean absolute error of 0.1617 and an R2 score of 0.354, showing that it can estimate dark matter fractions within approximately 16 percent of the actual calculated values.

The SHAP feature analysis revealed that flat rotation velocity and stellar mass are the two most important observable properties for determining a galaxy's dark matter fraction. Neutral hydrogen gas mass, baryonic mass, and effective surface brightness also provide secondary predictive value, while scale lengths, Hubble type, and color play smaller roles.

Astrophysically, these results show that a galaxy's total mass scale and rotation speed are the primary drivers of its dark matter fraction. Low-mass, gas-rich galaxies tend to be dominated by dark matter, whereas massive, high-surface-brightness spiral galaxies have lower relative dark matter fractions in their inner regions.